  "decision_status": "READY_TO_EXECUTE",
  "scientific_rationale": "PROP-003 tests the heterogeneity hypothesis (H3) that the association between commuting time and sleep time differs between women and men. Sleep showed the strongest negative point estimate in EXP-001, so assessing whether the association varies by sex addresses the key uncertainty. The proposal uses a formal interaction term, yielding an interval for the difference between groups, and the engine currently supports the required interaction capability, so it can be executed now.",
  "expected_learning": "If the interaction coefficient confidence interval excludes zero on the negative side, it indicates a more negative commuting–sleep association among women than men. If it excludes zero on the positive side, it indicates a more negative association among men than women. If the interval includes zero, the result is inconclusive, suggesting no clear difference in the association between sexes.",
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "proposal_id": "PROP-005",
      "reason_not_selected": "Provides only a sex‑specific coefficient without formally testing the difference, so it cannot establish heterogeneity and offers lower scientific rigor."
    },
    {
      "proposal_id": "PROP-008",
      "reason_not_selected": "Tests child‑presence moderation, which is valuable but secondary to the primary unresolved question about sex differences for the strongest outcome (sleep)."
    },
    {
      "proposal_id": "PROP-009",
      "reason_not_selected": "Examines sex heterogeneity for leisure, an outcome with a weaker negative point association than sleep, making it less informative for the key unresolved finding."
    }
  ],
